{
  "id": "decode-string",
  "questionId": "decode-string",
  "slug": "decode-string",
  "title": "Decode String",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Stack",
  "patterns": [
    "stack",
    "recursion"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "nested brackets mean you need to 'pause' building the current level's string whenever you enter a deeper bracket, then resume once it closes - what structure naturally supports pause and resume in reverse order?",
    "walk through s character by character; accumulate digits into a number, and accumulate letters into a current string buffer",
    "when you hit '[', push the current buffer and the number you just built onto a stack, then reset both to start building the inner content fresh",
    "when you hit ']', pop the (previous buffer, repeat count) pair, and set the current buffer to previous buffer + (current buffer repeated that many times)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting to reset the digit accumulator after pushing it on '[', causing digits from different levels to bleed together",
    "only handling single-digit repeat counts instead of accumulating multi-digit numbers",
    "concatenating in the wrong order when popping (appending the popped prefix after the repeated inner string instead of before)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an encoded string `s` containing lowercase letters, digits,\nand square brackets, following the pattern k[encoded_string], which means\nencoded_string should be repeated k times in the decoded output. The\nencoding can be nested, for example 3[a2[c]] means \"a2[c]\" repeated 3\ntimes, and 2[c] inside that means \"c\" repeated 2 times.\n\n**Return the fully decoded string.**\n\nYou can assume the input is always validly formatted and that k is always a positive integer with no leading zeroes.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= s.length <= 30",
      "s is guaranteed to be a validly formatted encoding with matched brackets",
      "k is always a positive integer with no leading zeroes",
      "the decoded output length fits well within a 32-bit integer"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "no brackets at all (plain letters, returned unchanged)",
      "multi-digit repeat counts (e.g. 10[a])",
      "deeply nested brackets several levels deep"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"3[a]2[bc]\"",
        "output": "\"aaabcbc\"",
        "expectedOutput": "\"aaabcbc\"",
        "explanation": "3[a] becomes \"aaa\", 2[bc] becomes \"bcbc\", concatenated"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"3[a2[c]]\"",
        "output": "\"accaccacc\"",
        "expectedOutput": "\"accaccacc\"",
        "explanation": "the inner 2[c] becomes \"cc\", giving \"acc\" inside the brackets, then that whole thing repeats 3 times"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"2[abc]3[cd]ef\"",
        "output": "\"abcabccdcdcdef\"",
        "expectedOutput": "\"abcabccdcdcdef\"",
        "explanation": "2[abc] becomes \"abcabc\", 3[cd] becomes \"cdcdcd\", then \"ef\" is appended unchanged"
      }
    ],
